{{also|Magenta|maġenta|magentą}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|fr|magenta}}, from {{der|en|it|Magenta}}, sometime after the colour was named after the town to celebrate the Franco-Italian victory at the {{w|Battle of Magenta}} in 1859; possibly in reference to the colour of the uniforms worn by [[Zouave]] French troops there. The town's name derives from {{der|en|la|[[castrum]] [[Maxentiae]]||castle of Maxentius}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/məˈd͡ʒɛntə/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-magenta.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/meˈd͡ʒanʈaː/|/-t̪aː/|a=Indic|a2=mainly <<Tanglish>>, also}}
* {{hyph|en|ma|gen|ta}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A color which is close to an equal mixture of red and blue which is an [[additive]] [[secondary]] color but a [[subtractive]] [[primary]] color evoked by the combination of red and blue light.
#: {{color panel|web magenta (additive magenta)|FF00FF}}
#: {{color panel|printer's magenta (subtractive magenta)|FF0090}}
#: {{color panel|Prismacolor magenta|D31185}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1950|author=Norman Lindsay|authorlink=Norman Lindsay|title={{w|Dust or Polish?}}|publisher=Angus and Robertson|location=Sydney|page=154|passage=Mrs Dibble's face blazed with a '''magenta''' uprush of blood at that ultimatum and she screamed, "You dare try to do me out of my gin too! You dare!"}}

